Blocks the Payflow Pro transparent redirect response endpoint while the payment method is disabled.

Root cause: Magento\Paypal\Controller\Transparent\Response is CSRF-exempt (validateForCsrf() returns
true) and stays routed whenever Magento_Paypal is enabled, regardless of whether Payflow Pro is active.
Its sibling controller, Magento\Paypal\Controller\Transparent\RequestSecureToken, already refuses the
request when $this->transparent->isActive() is false, but the equivalent guard is missing here.

Any unauthenticated POST therefore reaches ResponseValidator::validate(), whose default branch throws
"Transaction has been declined" for every input that is not a valid Payflow response. That exception is
caught and passed to PaymentFailuresInterface::handle(), which sends an admin "Payment Failed" email
rendered from the attacker-controlled quote billing address - once per request, with no rate limiting
and no captcha. The endpoint is abused in the wild both to flood shop mailboxes and to deliver
CVE-2022-24086 style template-injection payloads into that email render.

Fix: apply the same guard the sibling controller already uses, returning the standard 404 through
_forward('noroute') when payment/payflowpro/active is off. Shops that genuinely run Payflow Pro are
unaffected, and no new constructor dependency is required because the Transparent model is already
injected into the controller.

diff --git a/Controller/Transparent/Response.php b/Controller/Transparent/Response.php
index d5d4cf2..ae10689 100644
--- a/Controller/Transparent/Response.php
+++ b/Controller/Transparent/Response.php
@@ -118,6 +118,13 @@ class Response extends \Magento\Framework\App\Action\Action implements CsrfAware
      */
     public function execute()
     {
+        // BEGIN PATCH
+        if (!$this->transparent->isActive()) {
+            $this->_forward('noroute');
+
+            return;
+        }
+        // END PATCH
